WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.063
Some Google Cloud CLI actions are higher
impact because they can quickly change

2
00:00:09.143 --> 00:00:16.811
security posture, data exposure,
availability, or recovery options.

3
00:00:16.891 --> 00:00:24.815
IAM changes, service account changes,
network changes, storage access,

4
00:00:24.895 --> 00:00:32.052
encryption settings, KMS configuration,
Secret Manager access,

5
00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:38.267
compute changes, Kubernetes changes,
logging changes,

6
00:00:38.347 --> 00:00:47.932
and data-access changes deserve stronger
guardrails than ordinary read-only queries.

7
00:00:48.012 --> 00:00:56.830
Organization Policy and governance
controls can provide important guardrails,

8
00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:04.067
but scripts should not treat policy as a
reason to skip validation.

9
00:01:04.147 --> 00:01:11.944
Where Google Cloud services or deployment
workflows support previews,

10
00:01:12.024 --> 00:01:19.181
dry-run concepts, policy evaluation,
diffs, validation checks,

11
00:01:19.261 --> 00:01:27.057
or staged deployment, use those mechanisms
as defensive review points.

12
00:01:27.137 --> 00:01:35.955
They help reviewers understand the
effective change before a high-impact action

13
00:01:36.035 --> 00:01:38.336
reaches production.

14
00:01:38.416 --> 00:01:45.317
Deletion, overwrite, public exposure,
permission expansion,

15
00:01:45.397 --> 00:01:50.126
and encryption changes deserve extra care.

16
00:01:50.206 --> 00:01:58.641
A helper should require target validation,
release context, approval gates,

17
00:01:58.721 --> 00:02:08.051
operator confirmation, or change tickets
when the operation can affect production or

18
00:02:08.131 --> 00:02:10.559
sensitive resources.

19
00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:19.713
Ambiguous resource selection should fail
closed rather than let a broad command act

20
00:02:19.793 --> 00:02:22.605
on unintended resources.

21
00:02:22.685 --> 00:02:32.142
Guardrails should support idempotent
behavior, rollback thinking, and safe failure.

22
00:02:32.222 --> 00:02:40.402
A script should be designed so repeated
runs do not create uncontrolled side

23
00:02:40.482 --> 00:02:41.504
effects.

24
00:02:41.584 --> 00:02:48.358
Recovery paths should be considered before
the change begins,

25
00:02:48.438 --> 00:02:55.595
especially for identity, storage, data,
and networking changes.

26
00:02:55.675 --> 00:03:03.471
Secure Google Cloud CLI automation makes
high-impact changes visible,

27
00:03:03.551 --> 00:03:10.580
reviewed, bounded, and recoverable where
recovery is possible.
